  "projects": {
    "2017": [
      {
        "title": "Maximal static expansion for efficient loop parallelization on GPU",
        "short_description": "Polly is a high-level loop and data-locality optimizer and optimization infrastructure for LLVM based on the polyhedral model. This project aims to...",
        "description": "<p>Polly is a high-level loop and data-locality optimizer and optimization infrastructure for LLVM based on the polyhedral model. This project aims to implement a mechanism of static expansion in Polly in oder to parallelize loop that can not be directly parallelized because of data dependencies.</p>\n",
        "student_name": "Nicolas Bonfante",
        "code_url": "http://pollylabs.org/gsoc2017/Maximal-static-expansion-for-efficient-loop-parallelization-on-GPU.html",
        "project_url": "https://summerofcode.withgoogle.com/archive/2017/projects/5511723209457664/"
      }
    ],
    "2018": [
      {
        "title": "Collect Polyhedral Benchmarks",
        "short_description": "Currently, Polly’s performance is measured on PolyBench benchmark. PolyBench does not cover all of the possible kernels so we may miss out some...",
        "description": "<p>Currently, Polly’s performance is measured on PolyBench benchmark. PolyBench does not cover all of the possible kernels so we may miss out some kernels where a particular transformation does not perform well. This project will help to get new benchmarks for measuring Polly’s performance benefits. The benchmarks will be added to LLVM test suite.</p>\n",
        "student_name": "Pankaj Kukreja",
        "code_url": "http://pollylabs.org/gsoc2018/Collect-Polyhedral-Benchmarks.html",
        "project_url": "https://summerofcode.withgoogle.com/archive/2018/projects/6247142943358976/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Metamorphic Testing for Math Libraries",
        "short_description": "Recently, metamorphic testing techniques have been applied with success in\nvarious fields to find domain-specific bugs, as presented in this survey....",
        "description": "<p>Recently, metamorphic testing techniques have been applied with success in\nvarious fields to find domain-specific bugs, as presented in <a href=\"http://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/7422146/\" target=\"_blank\">this survey</a>.\nOne field yet to be explored is that of programming libraries, which comes with\nits own set of challenges. The existance of multiple libraries means we have a\nrich set of equivalence relations we can employ for metamorphic\nrelations, allowing extensive testing using this approach. This project aims to\nexplore the suitability of applying metamorphic testing to these math libraries\nand evaluate their reliability and correctness.</p>\n",
        "student_name": "Andrei Lascu",
        "code_url": "http://pollylabs.org/gsoc2018/Metamorphic-Testing-for-C++-Software-Libraries.html",
        "project_url": "https://summerofcode.withgoogle.com/archive/2018/projects/4798352227041280/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Compiling Chapel with Polly/LLVM",
        "short_description": "Chapel is an emerging parallel programming language developed with the aim of providing better performance in High-Performance Computing as well as...",
        "description": "<p>Chapel is an emerging parallel programming language developed with the aim of providing better performance in High-Performance Computing as well as accessibility to the newcomer programmers in order to provide a relatively smoother learning curve in Parallel Computing.</p>\n<p>Polly, a polyhedral compilation based optimization tool, performs data-locality based optimizations and automatic parallelization of LLVM IR represented in Polyhedral format. The resultant polyhedra are exposed to a number of loop optimizations like tiling, vectorization and strip mining.</p>\n<p>I propose to integrate Polly’s Optimization passes in Chapel. In today’s world, it is well known that High-Performance Computing involves nested loops as one of their most compute intensive parts which are efficiently handled by Polly-based optimizations, hereby making HPC more productive and going towards more compute-intensive problems which are currently unfeasible.</p>\n",
        "student_name": "Sahil Yerawar",
        "code_url": "http://pollylabs.org/gsoc2018/Compiling-Chapel-with-Polly-and-LLVM.html",
        "project_url": "https://summerofcode.withgoogle.com/archive/2018/projects/6559623557414912/"
      }
    ]
  },
